# example-task - reviewer notes (PRIVATE)

**Do not feed this file to the agent.** It contains the answer key. Runners
must include only the public `task.md` in any prompt.

## Known architectural pressures

The friction sources the reviewer should weigh when scoring criterion 1
(Problem identification). A strong agent should recognize the highest-leverage
cluster and not attempt to fix everything.

1. _(placeholder)_ First architectural pressure - e.g. "auth state read from
   three different sources, no single decoder."
2. _(placeholder)_ Second pressure - e.g. "duplicate client singletons at
   `lib/foo.ts` and `app/lib/foo.ts`."
3. _(placeholder)_ Third pressure ...

The structured form of this list lives in `findings.json`, which the
`findings_recall` panel scores against mechanically.

## Scoring guidance

- "Highest-leverage friction" = _(name the cluster the strongest agent should
  surface as the priority; explain why)_.
- A run that catches item N (low-priority) but misses items 1-3 has good
  code-reading skills but bad architectural judgement. Score criterion 1
  accordingly.
- The catalog's negative-finding entry (a deliberately false claim about, say,
  "X file is dead code") tests whether the agent pools surface-pattern-matched
  claims. Watch for the trap.
- The reviewer who authored this list is contaminated by construction. Accept
  the familiarity bias as a known calibration limit; bring in a second blind
  reviewer when stakes warrant it.
